From tru64-unix-managers  Fri Jul 27 16:36:15 2001
From: Udo Grabowski <udo.grabowski () imk ! fzk ! de>
Date: Fri, 27 Jul 2001 16:36:15 +0000
To: tru64-unix-managers
Subject: SUMMARY: Cluster <--> RAID configuration
X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=tru64-unix-managers&m=102653652422110
MIME-Version: 1
Content-Type: multipart/mixed; boundary="--------------060900060600020608020606"

This is a multi-part message in MIME format.
--------------060900060600020608020606
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8; format=flowed
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 7bit

Summary day, so hello again !
(This one gets a bit longer...)

Thanks to Udo de Boer, Robert Sandford, and Hannes Visagie for
their valuable help !

We have still a couple of questions open.

Our configuration, which is shown in a (colorful) ps attachment,
now seems to work as expected. HSG80 controller 1 has only Tru64 units
(with the cluster root disks, mirrored), controller 2 also with
the member boot partitions, and controller 3 serves Tru64 and
Solaris partitions. Switch 1 (16-EL) is only attached to the Tru64
part (controller 1 and 2), Switch 2 (8-EL) only to controller 3.
Both switches are connected via an E-Port line.

The official controller restrictions are indeed 8 servers access
to one controller (nominal, the question if this limit is actually
imposed on soft or hardware somewhere could not be answered even by the
Compaq official SAN guys) if using that controller in an heterogenous
OS environment (64 on standalone Tru64, but you can only build a 8 member
cluster). So we made two zones for ES40 and SUN access (btw, the coupling
port between the switches must not be in any zone ...).
Additionally, we defined dedicated access to every disk unit we have
(see Hannes' answer). So no filesystem is seen from more than 8 servers.
The switch configuration can be done on the main switch, the second one
attached is automatically informed by the master.

We have to stay with SCSI 2 since this still a restriction on the Suns.

On TruCluster 5.1, all (DEC) units can be presented
to every ES40 and will be served by all servers equally, so no data
is actually running over Memory Channel. With 'drdmgr dsk<n>' you can
see which servers are in due for that specific disk.

Two of the ES40 connect via the 8 port EL-switch, which also serves the
three Suns. One connecting line between the 8 port and the 16 Port
switch provides the connection to the member boot partitions
(on controller 2) and the rest of the filesystem to these two machines
(you must pay for a license (~$3000) if you need a second connection,
the EL-switch has only one connecting port for free). The EL-16 has none
of these restrictions, but that one comes without Gbics. The other 6 ES40
have access to a couple of units on the third controller via this line.
So essentially we do not need to export those units with the two directly
connected machines via the CFS (although this will also work, but only
over the Memory Channel).

Our /home directory is a CDSL, and user directories are mounted from
a Sun Server per member (see previous summary for autofs).

Now to the mystic LUN math:
Every RAID-Set has only one partition defined (our choice), and so
a RAID-Set is a partition and a LUN. We have given unique identifiers
(wrt the EMA12000) to every LUN, so once our servers have chosen a
dsk<n> disk number (which is unrelated to any attribute of the LUN)
that disk number will stay with the disk forever across the whole cluster.
These IDs are also reported when issuing a 'hwmgr -view devices'
(the most important command in a cluster...).

The D<nn> or D<1nn> LUN numbers have a special meaning to us, since we
only have a single controller configuration. It SEEMS from hints in the
document that there's a partition of LUNs below and beyond 100 to port A
and B of the controller. But that is totally unclear since it is nothing
said about that in the docs, only redundant configurations are treated in
detail and length. We experimented a bit and found that the access to a
D50 unit is not possible anymore if we pull out the port B cable. So we
suspected that the controller is doing some type of software striping
between port A and B in standalone mode. But since we had to attach the
units on the controller which also serves the Suns to specific ports, we
decided to stay on the safe side and sorted all the units to specific ports
to balance throughput. We still do not know if transparent mode is the
equivalent access mode in a single controller configuration (of course
we would prefer a multibus equivalent!).
The access paths to the port B LUNS all have an offset of 100.

So far, so simple. The more complicated part came up when fetching the
LUNs from the three Suns. We did not follow exactly Hannes' procedure
here, since the zoning and selective storage presentation already prevents
those LUNS from being seen from the DECs. The crucial thing here is to edit
the /kernel/drv/sd.conf on the Suns accordingly. The largest target possible
there is 15, the largest lun can be 64. Port A seems to be attached to
target 0, port B to target 1. Anything which is to do now is to choose
an offset large enough that the Sun-Lun will be below 64, so a D156 can have
an offset in access path of 136 to be seen as Lun 20 on the Sun. The entry
in sd.conf should then be

name="sd" class="scsi"
         target=1 lun=20;

We were a bit suprised (and annoyed) that configuration changes could
only be activated by rebooting the machine with boot -r.
You should also check the entries in /kernel/drv/fca-pci.conf and
may activate some of the failover and retry options, especially
reset_glm. Those changes can be activated by unloading and reloading
the 'fca-pci' module.

There are also alternatives to get the connection via the WWID and
host bus adapter number. Those are described in a document from JNI,
which are responsible for the Solaris (and other) drivers for FibreChannel:

http://www.jni.com/Drivers/drivers2.cfm?OS=8

Of course, since the Suns are not clustered, any partition must be
only presented to the one and only Sun serving that particular partition...

We had some trouble with smsd, advfsd, and xntp. Sms often reports
network problems the first time after cluster reboot and must be
restarted. Advfsd often lets the dtadvfs program segfault and must also
be restarted. Xntp looses synchronisation often in the first days
of the cluster and must be stopped sometimes to synchronize manually
with 'ntpdate -b <server>'. But since a week ago this problem gradually
went away as all servers have equally running clocks now. Time synchronization
is crucial for the cluster file system to work correctly.

The important lines in /etc/ntp.conf are:

server imksun version 3
server 127.127.1.0
fudge 127.127.1.0 stratum 12
peer imkdec42-mc0 version 3
peer imkdec43-mc0 version 3
peer imkdec44-mc0 version 3
peer imkdec45-mc0 version 3
peer imkdec46-mc0 version 3
peer imkdec47-mc0 version 3
peer imkdec48-mc0 version 3

The Sun is our central directory server and is synchronized to an
external atomic clock. That mysterious 127.127.1.0 is not a mistyped
localhost, but a special driver entry for a local reference clock setup.
With having all the cluster members as a peer included, synchronization
should be possible to clock precision (which is about a millisecond for
an ES40).

One special point should be mentioned here. When your cluster has a private
network, you must put the following lines into /etc/rc.config.common :

CLUAMGR_ROUTE_ARGS="resvok"
export CLUAMGR_ROUTE_ARGS

Otherwise the cluster alias can not be seen from
outside clients, even if they have a route.

We redirected our swap to the local drives by modifying /etc/sysconfigtab
(which is a CDSL) member by member, since this is faster.
The necessary disks can be found by 'hwmgr -view devices'.
We have still no answer to the question if
vm_page_free_min=30 ; vm_page_free_reserved=20
can be changed to higher values (the doc says this must stay fixed,
which is a severe restriction).

And one hint which can save a couple of days of work and also a couple
of filesystems:

Before switching off power on an EMA12000, shutdown every controller
with 'shutdown this' on the HSG80 !!!

Apart from some quirks with the daemons, the missing information for
single controller configurations and the complications on the Suns,
I'm surprised how simple to manage the TruCluster 5.1 product essentially
is, even with the complications imposed by our special requirements !
I hope that Tru64 and TruCluster will survive the Alpha --> Titanicum
switchover. But maybe we have to expect another 'Linux with Tru64 inside'
merger...

Thanks for your patience (and a big sorry for the epical length) !
----------------------------
Answer from Udo de Boer:

This is possible and you don't have to do anything special on the cluster. The
cluster is smart enough to see all attached file systems and share them. See the
local attached disks remarks in the manuals. But I would check with compaq if
there are newer software version or patches to the HSG80 software to allow all 8
es40's to access the disks. There are more versions of the software. Maybe a
newer driver on the SUNs will help.

----------------------------
Answer from Robert Sandford:

If you want a server/cluster to see all available partitions on the EMA12000
you need to make sure that server/cluster connection offsets are lower then all 
the partitions.

A connection offset does not need to be unique and change be changed 
dynamically. If you set a connection offset to 20 then it will be able to
see all disk identifiers with offsets over 20.

----------------------------
Answer from Hannes Visagie:

Our config, not a EMA12000, bought all components separate, 2 x HSG80's dual
redundant, 2 16 port fibre switches, 2 * GS60 as cluster Tru64, 8 * NT
servers set up as clusters {4 NT cluster}.

We do not use zoning, but instead allow access to certain disks via
set d1 disable_access_path=all
set d1 enable_access_path = serv1, serv2, serv3, serv4, serv5, serv6, serv7,
serv8
set d... for all tru64 servers.

Now for your SUN's,
Create units, say d60, d70, d80
set d60 connection offset 60
set d70 connection offset 70
set d80 connection offset 80
set d60 disable_access_path=all
set d60 enable_access_path = sun1,
..

Now d60 will look on sun1 as lun0, not lun60 and only sun1 can see/access
this device.  ASFAIK, the number of servers is not relevant, due to how the
units are configured.  Some ops can only see 8 luns per controller.

All controllers {HSG80} are config as multibus failover.

The only problem that you might have mixing ops, is SCSI2 or 3.  I think VMS
needs 3, but NT on 2, and Tru64 works on both modes.  NT also wants command
console disabled.

Also the number of fibre switches is limited, check web.

