From tru64-unix-managers  Fri Jul 13 16:53:51 2001
From: Arrigo Triulzi <arrigo () albourne ! com>
Date: Fri, 13 Jul 2001 16:53:51 +0000
To: tru64-unix-managers
Subject: [SUMMARY] Interaction between NHD kit and 4.0F PK solved?
X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=tru64-unix-managers&m=102653652421862

The answer, from Dr. Tom Blinn (and who else?) is that apparently it
is fixed. 

A very useful suggestion was:

`` 
  The easiest way to tell is to look at the patch inventory files
  seeking out the "ee" references; if they are in the same locations
  as the equivalent files delivered by the NHD3 kit, then it's fixed.
  In particular, look for the "files" file fragments; that's where the
  problem was, not in the ee.mod (which has to go into the /sys/BINARY
  directory).  
''

which undoubtedly quite a few people will find worth applying.

Ciao,

Arrigo

From tru64-unix-managers  Fri Jul 13 16:51:02 2001
From: Arrigo Triulzi <arrigo () albourne ! com>
Date: Fri, 13 Jul 2001 16:51:02 +0000
To: tru64-unix-managers
Subject: [SUMMARY] Machine check error on DS20E - decode?
X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=tru64-unix-managers&m=102653652421861

Thanks to Alan Davis and Dr. Tom Blinn for their responses.  I've been
holding back to see if anything else was forthcoming.

Basically the answer is that DIA does not support the DS20E so it will
not auto-decode and from a dump there is very little which can be done
unless you are the person who wrote that bit of the kernel dump code
(Tom Blinn was slightly more "emphatic" about my wishful thinking).

They both suggest logging a call with Compaq.  The reason I haven't
done so yet is that the local support people sometimes need guidance
and being able to point them to the correct source of the problem
avoids taking the machine up and down like a yo-yo while they try
things out.   Since the box is up and in production I'd rather this
didn't happen.  Still, it needs a fix so I will have to call them in
and suffer.

Ciao,

Arrigo

