From tru64-unix-managers  Tue Jul 10 04:36:11 2001
From: Jerome M Berkman <jerry () uclink ! berkeley ! edu>
Date: Tue, 10 Jul 2001 04:36:11 +0000
To: tru64-unix-managers
Subject: 32k limit to a directory?
X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=tru64-unix-managers&m=102653652421758

Ian Veach wrote that Chris Adams reminded us that there are
32K directory limitations that require workarounds ...
	 
We will soon have a directory with about 40,000 entries (for a product
for which we don't have source), so this worries me.

We are currently running DU 4.0F using ADVFS.  I just ran a simple
script (see below) to create 75,000 files in a single directory,
and the only problem was speed.  It takes roughly twice the system
time to create the 50,000th file as the 25,000th file.  I've heard
Tru64 5.1 will solve this.  Are there other problems with directories
with large numbers of files?

	- Jerry Berkman, UC Berkeley, jerry@uclink.berkeley.edu

#!      /usr/local/bin/perl -Tw

#       see how many files I can put in a directory and how long it takes

$| = 1;

for( $i = 1; $i <= 100; $i++ ) {
        doit( sprintf( "file%3.3d", $i ) );
}
exit 0;

sub doit {
        my( $init ) = @_;
        my( $ts_begin, $ts_end, $elapsed, $i );

        $ts_begin = time;
        ($ucpu0,$scpu0,$uc0,$sc0) = times();
        for( $i = 0; $i <= 999; $i++ ) {
                $name = sprintf( "$init.%3.3d", $i );
                open( FILE, "> /var/tmp/big/$name" ) or die "can't write $name";
                print FILE "sample line for $name\n";
                close FILE;
        }
        $ts_end = time;
        ($ucpu1,$scpu1,$uc1,$sc1) = times();
        $ucpu = sprintf( "%3.1f", $ucpu1 - $ucpu0);
        $scpu = sprintf( "%3.1f", $scpu1 - $scpu0);
        $elapsed = $ts_end - $ts_begin;
        print "$init took $ucpu u, $scpu s, $elapsed elapsed\n";
}

From tru64-unix-managers  Tue Jul 10 03:04:54 2001
From: Jarek Wieczorek <jarek () allinfo ! com>
Date: Tue, 10 Jul 2001 03:04:54 +0000
To: tru64-unix-managers
Subject: [SUMMARY] AIT tape drive errors
X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=tru64-unix-managers&m=102653652421757

Hello;

I got plenty of suggestions from people on the list. Special thanx to 
alan@nabeth.cxo.dec.com, Selden E Ball Jr (seb@lns62.lns.cornell.edu), and 
Thomas Blinn (tpb@doctor.zk3.dec.com) for general SCSI info and other 
specific issues.

The original problem was that from time to time I got errors when using the 
drive. tepex usually failed as well somewhere along its way, too (usually 
during "Writing and reading past the end of media" test). I did not know if 
it drive drive, SCSI controller or configuration problem.

Armed with knowledge suplied by above people I played with any possible, 
software, and hardware configuration (switches, cabling, IDs, parameters, 
etc.) Finally it occurred that the SCSI controller 3X-KZPCA-AA does NOT 
support configuration when it is in the middle of a SCSI bus (devices are 
connected both to external and internal connectors of the controller). But it 
had not stop the controller's manufacturer from including detailed directions 
how to put it into such a config. My advise is - do not try it at home. So I 
bought a short internal SCSI cable, a short external SCSI cable, and a SCSI 
sex changer (hard to get). Then I mounted the sex changer at one of SCSI 
breaks-troughs at the back of the machine, run the internal cable from disk 
cage SCSI output to the sex changer, and connected the library to the 
external side of the sex changer. It is fine now.

Jarek.



